<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

    <worksheet margin="1in" bottom="1.25cm" xml:id="normal-expectation-complete">
    <title>Normalization and Expectation (Complete)</title>
        <page>
        
            <exercise workspace="2in">
            <title>Motivation</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        We've seen the Born interpretation leads to probability distribution functions <m>|\psi|^2 = \overline{\psi} \cdot \psi</m>.
                        Now we'll see two common ways these functions are utilized in conjunction with improper integrals.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>

            <exercise workspace="3in">
            <title>Normalization</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        If <m>f(x)</m> represents a valid probability distribution, it must satisfy
                        <md>
                            \int_{-\infty}^\infty f(x) = 
                        </md>
                        
                         
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        <m>1</m>. That is, the probability that the particle is found in all of space is <m>100\%</m>. This condition
                        is often used to us determine values of parameters within a function <m>f(x)</m> to make it a valid 
                        probability distribution function (called <term>normalizing</term> <m>f(x)</m>).
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>

            <exercise>
                <title>Examples: Normalizing Functions</title>
                <task workspace="3in">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Let 
                            <md>
                                f(x) = \begin{cases}
                                    c \amp \text{if } 1 \leq x \leq 10, \\
                                    0  \amp \text{otherwise}.
                                    \end{cases}
                            </md>
                            
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            <m>\int_{-\infty}^\infty f(x) \quad dx = 1</m> implies that <m>\int_1^{10} c \quad dx = 1</m>. Using the
                            FTC, this implies that <m>10c - c = 1</m>, so that <m>c = 1/9</m>.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            The function <m>f(x)</m> here is called a <term>uniform distribution</term>. In general, uniform distributions take the form
                            <md>
                                f(x) = \begin{cases}
                                    \frac{1}{b-a} \amp \text{if } a \leq x \leq b, \\
                                    0  \amp \text{otherwise}.
                                    \end{cases}
                            </md>
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </task>

                <task workspace="3in">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Let <m>g(x) = ce^{-x^2}</m>.
                            
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            Note that <m>\int_{-\infty}^\infty g(x) \quad dx = c\int_{-\infty}^\infty e^{-x^2} \quad dx = c \sqrt{\pi}</m>.
                            Therefore, normalizing <m>g(x)</m> would require setting <m>c = \frac{1}{\sqrt{\pi}}</m>.
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </task>
                
            </exercise>
    
    </page>
    <page>

            <exercise workspace="1in">
                <title>Expectation</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Consider a particle which can be at any one of four positions at a given time with the listed 
                        probabilities:
                    
                    </p>
           
  
                            <tabular top="major" bottom="minor">
                                <row header="yes" bottom="major">
                                <cell><m>x</m></cell>
                                <cell><m>p(x)</m></cell>
                                </row>
                                <row>
                                <cell><m>1</m></cell>
                                <cell><m>0.1</m></cell>
                                </row>
                                <row>
                                <cell><m>2</m></cell>
                                <cell><m>0.6</m></cell>
                                </row>
                                <row>
                                <cell><m>3</m></cell>
                                <cell><m>0.2</m></cell>
                                </row>
                                <row>
                                <cell><m>4</m></cell>
                                <cell><m>0.1</m></cell>
                                </row>
                            </tabular>
                <p>
                    If we could observe this experiment for several trials, how could we describe the expected position 
                    of the particle? 
                </p>

                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        We could use a weighted average:
                        <md>
                            1(0.1) + 2(0.6) + 3(0.2) + 4(0.1) = 2.3
                        </md>.
                        Note that this is not a measure of the value we'd see most often (<m>2.3</m> is not even a valid position 
                        for the particle in this example), but a measure of the central tendency (average) of multiple observations.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        For a continuous probability distribution function <m>f(x)</m>, this leads to the definition
                        <md>
                            \text{Expectation (Average Value)} = \int_{-\infty}^\infty xf(x) \quad dx
                        </md>.
                        
                    </p>
                </solution>
              
                
            </exercise>

            
    </page>
    <page>
        <exercise>
            <title>Examples: Expectation</title>
            <task workspace="4in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                    What is the expectation for the uniform distribution
                    <md>
                        f(x) = \begin{cases}
                                    \frac{1}{b-a} \amp \text{if } a \leq x \leq b, \\
                                    0  \amp \text{otherwise}
                                    \end{cases}
                    </md>?
                    
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        <md>
                            <mrow> \int_{-\infty}^\infty xf(x) \quad dx \amp= \int_a^b x \frac{1}{b-a} \quad dx </mrow>
                            <mrow> \amp= \frac{1}{2(b-a)}(b^2 - a^2) </mrow>
                            <mrow> \amp= \frac{1}{2}(b+a) </mrow>
                        </md>.
                        Note that in the case of the uniform distrbution, this is just the average of <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>

            <task workspace="4in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                    What is the expectation for the distribution <m>g(x) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{\pi}}e^{-x^2}</m>?
                
                    
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        First, verify that the expression to be integrated, <m>xg(x)</m>, is an odd function. Next, use technology
                        to confirm the integral converges. Finally, 
                        using symmetry we can determine that <m>\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} xg(x) \quad dx = 0</m>.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>
            
        </exercise>
    </page>

       
    </worksheet>
